'''Agamoidea''' (ag-a̤-moi′dē-ä), ''n. pl.'' [NL., < ''Agama''<sup>1</sup> + ''-oidea''.] A superfamily of eriglossate lacertilians, having concavo-convex vertebræ, clavicles not dilated proximally, and no postorbital or postfrontal squamosal arches. {{smaller|The group comprises the families ''Agamidæ, Iguanidæ, Xenosauridæ, Zonuridæ'', and ''Anguidæ''. See cuts under ''Agamidæ'' and ''Iguana''.}}

'''agamous''' (ag′a̤-mus), ''a.'' [< NL. ''agamus'', < Gr. ἄγαμος, without marriage, unmarried, < ἀ- priv. + γάμος, marriage.] '''1.''' In ''bot.'', same as ''agamic''. —'''2.''' In ''zoöl.'', having no distinguishable sexual organs. See ''agamic'', 1. [Rare.]
{{smaller block|The molluscan race are divided into two branches, the phanerogamous and the ''agamous'' or cryptogamic.
{{right|''Johnston'', Introd. to Conchol.}}}}

'''agamy''' (ag′a̤-mi), ''n.'' [< Gr. ἀγαμία, < ἄγαμος: see ''agamous''.] Non-marriage; abstention from marriage, or rejection or non-recognition of the requirement of marriage in the relation of the sexes.

'''aganglionic''' (a-gang-gli-on′ik), ''a.'' [< Gr. ἀ- priv. (''a-''<sup>18</sup>) + ''ganglionic''.] Characterized by the absence of ganglia.

'''agapæ''', ''n.'' Plural of ''agape''<sup>2</sup>.

'''Agapanthus''' (ag-a̤-pan′thus), ''n.'' [NL., < Gr. ἀγάπη, love (see ''agape''<sup>2</sup>), + ἄνθος, flower.] A small genus of ornamental plants belonging to the natural order ''Liliaceæ''. {{smaller|The species are perennial herbs from southern Africa, with strap-shaped radical leaves and large umbels of bright-blue flowers. They have been long in cultivation.}}

'''agape'''<sup>1</sup> (a̤-gäp′ or a̤-gāp′), ''prep. phr.'' as ''adv.'' or ''a.'' [< ''a''<sup>3</sup> + ''gape''.] With the mouth wide open; in an attitude of wonder, expectation, or eager at-

